WEBVTT

1
00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:14.037
"Look how big its feet are!" Eric pointed.

2
00:00:14.038 --> 00:00:16.606
"How did they find all the bones?"

3
00:00:16.607 --> 00:00:18.447
Dana raised her hand.

4
00:00:18.448 --> 00:00:22.616
All the kids in the class were looking at a dinosaur exhibit.

5
00:00:22.617 --> 00:00:24.853
"Do you want to know a secret?"

6
00:00:24.854 --> 00:00:28.724
Miss Bennett, the museum guide, said in a low voice.

7
00:00:28.725 --> 00:00:34.907
"Those are not real dinosaur bones!"

8
00:00:34.908 --> 00:00:38.980
"But . . . but . . . but," Eric stuttered.

9
00:00:38.981 --> 00:00:44.367
"So dinosaurs never existed?" Carol asked timidly.

10
00:00:44.368 --> 00:00:48.688
"Of course they existed!" Miss Bennett laughed.

11
00:00:48.689 --> 00:00:53.021
"But those bones are not the original bones.

12
00:00:53.022 --> 00:00:57.259
Follow me and I will explain."

13
00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:00.591
The class followed her into another room.

14
00:01:00.592 --> 00:01:06.581
"First, who knows where those bones came from?" Miss Bennett asked.

15
00:01:06.582 --> 00:01:10.771
"From dead dinosaurs?" Eric answered.

16
00:01:10.772 --> 00:01:13.887
"They come from fossils!" Carol said.

17
00:01:13.888 --> 00:01:16.182
"You are both right.

18
00:01:16.183 --> 00:01:21.926
The dinosaur's dead bones turned into fossils."

19
00:01:21.927 --> 00:01:24.769
Miss Bennett switched on a projector.

20
00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:27.921
She pointed at the dinosaur on the screen.

21
00:01:27.922 --> 00:01:29.947
"Look at this dinosaur.

22
00:01:29.948 --> 00:01:36.721
Usually, when a dinosaur dies, it decays or another animal eats it.

23
00:01:36.722 --> 00:01:45.393
But sometimes the dinosaur is buried before it gets destroyed.

24
00:01:45.394 --> 00:01:49.623
Slowly the flesh and skin of the dinosaur rot away.

25
00:01:49.624 --> 00:01:51.771
Only the bones are left.

26
00:01:51.772 --> 00:01:56.644
Then more and more mud and sand cover the bones.

27
00:01:56.645 --> 00:02:02.983
The mud and sand, which are called sediment, press down on the bones.

28
00:02:02.984 --> 00:02:05.413
The sediment has water in it.

29
00:02:05.414 --> 00:02:11.076
The water has minerals.

30
00:02:11.077 --> 00:02:14.670
The minerals soak into the dinosaur bones.

31
00:02:14.671 --> 00:02:19.568
Slowly the bone material is replaced by the minerals.

32
00:02:19.569 --> 00:02:22.197
The bones become hard rock.

33
00:02:22.198 --> 00:02:27.800
Now they are fossils.

34
00:02:27.801 --> 00:02:31.825
This process takes a very long time.

35
00:02:31.826 --> 00:02:37.995
It takes thousands and thousands of years for the sediment to form heavy layers.

36
00:02:37.996 --> 00:02:43.486
These heavy layers turn into sedimentary rock around the fossils.

37
00:02:43.487 --> 00:02:49.780
So most fossils are found in this rock."

38
00:02:49.781 --> 00:02:55.330
When the slide show was over, Miss Bennett gave everyone a slab of clay.

39
00:02:55.331 --> 00:02:58.219
"Let's try a simple experiment,"

40
00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:00.820
she said as she held up her hand.

41
00:03:00.821 --> 00:03:03.546
"Place your hand on the clay.

42
00:03:03.547 --> 00:03:10.380
Pretend that your hand is a dead dinosaur covered with sand and mud.

43
00:03:10.381 --> 00:03:17.275
Press down on your hand and imagine it is being covered by more layers of sand and mud.

44
00:03:17.276 --> 00:03:23.837
Imagine the sediment keeps pressing for thousands, even millions of years!"

45
00:03:23.838 --> 00:03:28.505
"Ouch! My hand hurts, Miss Bennett!" Eric said.

46
00:03:28.506 --> 00:03:36.375
Everyone laughed, and Miss Bennett continued her explanation.

47
00:03:36.376 --> 00:03:41.398
"It took many years for the dinosaur bones to become fossils.

48
00:03:41.399 --> 00:03:47.219
Finally scientists dug the fossils out of the sedimentary rock.

49
00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:51.624
They had to be very careful not to break the rock.

50
00:03:51.625 --> 00:03:57.301
After they cleaned the rock, what did they find?" asked Miss Bennett.

51
00:03:57.302 --> 00:04:00.980
"A fossil!" everybody answered.

52
00:04:00.981 --> 00:04:03.900
They all held up their slabs of clay.

53
00:04:03.901 --> 00:04:07.014
On each one was a handprint.

54
00:04:07.015 --> 00:04:13.174
"Your handprint is not your actual hand," said Miss Bennett.

55
00:04:13.175 --> 00:04:17.537
"But we can still see that it was a hand.

56
00:04:17.538 --> 00:04:24.355
In the same way, fossils are not the actual bones of a dinosaur."

57
00:04:24.356 --> 00:04:31.655
"Is that why you said that the dinosaur bones we saw are not real?" called out Eric.

58
00:04:31.656 --> 00:04:36.080
"Yes, you are right." Miss Bennett nodded.

59
00:04:36.081 --> 00:04:44.384
"Remember, the original bones are replaced by rocklike minerals," Miss Bennett explained.

60
00:04:44.385 --> 00:04:51.270
"So a fossil is just a model of the original bone, like your handprint.

61
00:04:51.271 --> 00:04:57.003
And the dinosaur bones in the exhibit are models of the fossils."

62
00:04:57.004 --> 00:05:01.189
"Thank you, Miss Bennett," the children said together.

63
00:05:01.190 --> 00:05:04.277
"We learned a lot today."

64
00:05:04.278 --> 00:05:08.188
"Come back anytime!" Miss Bennett waved.

65
00:05:08.189 --> 00:05:13.073
"There is still more to learn about the world of fossils."
